   "text": "I've completed the search and submitted my specification.\n\n**Summary of my approach:**\n\n1. **Declared triggers** before any moves: stop if best-so-far Sharpe exceeds 1.2, stop after 2 consecutive failures to improve, or restart if the last gain was negligible (\u22640.02).\n\n2. **Skipped `pick_prior`** \u2014 since the 40 candidate features were unlabeled/anonymous, I had no domain-independent reason to favor any particular one before seeing data.\n\n3. **First move: `init`** \u2014 anchored on the single best feature out of 80 candidates (40 features \u00d7 2 signs), yielding an in-sample Sharpe of **1.2151**.\n\n4. **Trigger fired immediately**: my `best_so_far_above(1.2)` rule fired on the very first move. Rather than treating this as license to keep pushing for a higher number (which would mean searching harder on the same data and inflating overfitting risk), I treated the fired stop rule as the genuine signal to halt \u2014 a single-feature specification is simpler and more likely to reflect a persistent property of the data than a tuned 3-feature combination would be.\n\n5. **Final specification**: `[feature 0, sign -]` (short the top-ranked feature), equal-weight (trivial with one feature).\n\n6. **Predicted OOS Sharpe**: mean **0.5**, sd **0.4** \u2014 reflecting that this Sharpe was the max over 80 candidates, so meaningful selection-bias decay is expected out-of-sample, while still crediting that a single robust feature is less fragile than an overfit multi-feature blend."
